window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] = window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] || {};
window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][79] = "<span id=\"app-e-iq-c24-07\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C24-07</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> ai · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C24 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AI · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Meta</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> An AI assistant will be added to a messaging app used by a billion people. How do you roll it out and what safety issues matter?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Launch to everyone with a disclaimer.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Stages the rollout and names safety issues.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Does the scale arithmetic, defines harm categories and evaluation, covers teens and privacy, sets reporting and kill switches, uses red-teaming and sampling, and plans markets and measures.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general; nothing here describes any real product. At this scale, rare failures become daily events: if 1,000,000,000 assistant conversations a day have a harmful output rate of 0.01% (one in 10,000), that is 100,000 harmful outputs a day; at 0.001% it is still 10,000. So the design aim is not zero errors but very low rates in the worst categories, fast detection and fast response. <em>Harm categories and evaluation.</em> Define them up front: dangerous advice (self-harm, weapons, medical), harassment and hate, sexual content (especially involving minors), misinformation on elections and health, privacy violations (revealing personal data), and manipulation or scams. For each: a test set, a target rate, and reviewers. Use red-teaming (people deliberately trying to make it fail) before launch and continuously. Sample live conversations (with privacy safeguards) for human review. <em>Privacy.</em> Messaging is personal. Be explicit about what the assistant sees (only messages sent to it, not private chats), how long data is kept, whether it is used for training, and controls to delete it. <em>Teens.</em> Stricter defaults for younger users: narrower topics, no romantic role-play, resources for crisis, parental controls where appropriate, and tested age signals; the harm cost is higher and the evaluation stricter. <em>Rollout.</em> Staged by market and by user group: employees, then a small share in a few markets, then wider, with the guardrails reviewed at each stage, and market-specific tests for languages and local sensitivities. A kill switch per feature and per harm category. <em>Response.</em> A reporting button in the conversation, a rapid-response team with authority to restrict behaviors within hours, and public transparency about limits. <em>Measures.</em> Harmful-output rates per category from sampled review, user reports per million conversations, time to mitigate, usefulness and satisfaction, and retention; guardrails on the harm rates for launch gates. <em>Company note.</em> Meta is the setting only: <code>Public</code> that Meta offers an assistant in its apps, <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports of assistant-at-scale prompts <code>[V: Meta: Meta AI assistant in Facebook, Instagram, WhatsApp and Messenger, sources through 2025; the prompt descriptions remain candidate-reported]</code>. No Meta data or practice is claimed.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c24-08\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C24-08</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> ai · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C24 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AI · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Netflix</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A team wants to use a language model to write personalized titles and synopses for shows. What do you consider?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Great idea; generate them automatically.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Raises accuracy, spoilers and brand voice.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Quantifies the spoiler or error risk, designs review proportional to risk, measures impact with a holdout rather than clicks alone, and considers creators' rights and consistency.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general; nothing here describes any real practice. Personalized descriptions could help members find shows they will like, and the risks are specific. <em>What can go wrong.</em> Spoilers (revealing plot twists), inaccurate descriptions (mismatched tone, wrong characters), misleading hooks that raise clicks and lower satisfaction, inconsistency with the creators' own words, and problems with sensitive themes. Suppose a test on 500 titles finds 3% of generated synopses contain a spoiler (invented): across a 15,000-title catalog, that is about 450 titles at risk if nothing is reviewed. <em>Review in proportion.</em> Reviewing every description would take 15,000 titles × 2 minutes = 500 hours, which is manageable for a one-time pass and not for every personalized variant. So: have editors approve a set of building blocks (approved facts, approved tone, a list of forbidden reveals per title) and let the model only rearrange and select from them; fully review new and high-profile releases; sample-audit the rest; and add an automatic check that compares each generated text against the title's list of spoilers. <em>Measurement.</em> Clicks measure curiosity, not value. Use qualified plays (starts that reach a meaningful point), completion, post-watch ratings, and long-term retention against a holdout, and compare with human-written descriptions. Watch for a rise in starts with a rise in early abandonment, which signals a misleading hook. <em>Rights and trust.</em> Creators and studios may have contractual say over how titles are described; respect it, and keep an audit trail of what was generated for whom. <em>Rollout.</em> Start with a small set of titles and members, shadow-test, and expand as spoiler rates and satisfaction hold. <em>Company note.</em> Netflix is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports of generated-content and personalization prompts <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Netflix data or practice is claimed.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c24-09\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C24-09</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> ai · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C24 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AI · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Stripe</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Design an AI assistant for developers that answers questions from the documentation and can see their account. What are the risks?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Feed the docs and their account data to the model.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Uses grounding and raises secrets and permissions.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Handles API versions, tests generated code, protects keys and account data, treats user content as untrusted, measures the hallucinated-endpoint rate and the cost of wrong answers, and scopes account actions.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general; nothing here describes any real system. Developers ask how to do something; wrong answers cost them time and can cost money. <em>Grounding and versions.</em> Answer from the documentation, with citations, and from the version of the API the developer's account uses; documentation for an older or newer version produces code that fails. Retrieval metadata should include the version, and the assistant should state which version it assumes. <em>The cost of wrong answers.</em> Suppose 1,000,000 questions a month and 5% contain a hallucinated endpoint or parameter (invented), each costing the developer half an hour: 50,000 wrong answers and 25,000 hours a month. Grounding and checks that cut it to 1% reduce that to 10,000 answers and 5,000 hours. The measure to track is the rate of answers whose code or endpoint does not exist or does not work. <em>Test generated code.</em> Where possible, run generated snippets in a sandbox against the real API schema before showing them, and check that every endpoint and parameter named exists; drop or flag answers that fail. <em>Secrets and permissions.</em> Never put API secret keys into the model's context; if the assistant needs account information, give it scoped, read-only access to specific, non-secret fields (recent errors, configuration), enforced by the product, not by the prompt. It must never reveal another account's data. <em>Injection.</em> Developers' accounts contain text that outsiders can influence (a customer's name, a webhook payload, a support message). If the assistant reads it, an attacker could embed instructions. Treat all account content as data, restrict the assistant to read-only actions, and require explicit confirmation for anything that changes configuration. <em>Interface.</em> Cite the doc passage, show the version, make code copyable with a note on what was checked, and offer &quot;report a wrong answer&quot; with the context attached. <em>Measures.</em> Hallucinated-endpoint rate, code that runs when copied, deflected support tickets, developer satisfaction, and incidents involving data exposure (target zero). <em>Company note.</em> Stripe is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports of developer-assistant prompts <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Stripe data or design is claimed.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c24-10\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C24-10</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> ai · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C24 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AI · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> When would you decide not to use AI for a feature?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;When it's too expensive.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names cases where rules suffice or errors are too costly.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Applies a structured fit test with the 10% error question, considers evaluation, explainability, user control and cost per use, and offers a rule-based or hybrid alternative.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd run a short fit test (FW-C24-01) and decline when any of these is true and cannot be fixed with design. <em>A rule does the job.</em> Validating a date, calculating a fee or tax from a table, routing by a known category: a rule is exact, cheap, fast and easy to audit. A model would add errors and cost. <em>The cost of an error is high and unchecked.</em> A medication dose, a legal filing, moving money, anything where a wrong answer causes serious harm and neither the user nor a reviewer can catch it. In those cases AI may still assist (drafting, summarizing) with a person deciding, but not decide. <em>Explanation or audit is required.</em> If a regulator or a customer must be told exactly why a decision was made, a model's reasoning is hard to certify. <em>I cannot evaluate it.</em> If I have no examples and no way to judge outputs, I would be shipping blind; I would first build the evaluation. <em>Cost per use exceeds value.</em> At $0.007 a request, an assistant that adds one cent of value is unprofitable at scale. <em>Users want control.</em> A clear filter or search can beat a clever guess for people who know what they want. <em>My simple question:</em> if this feature were wrong 10% of the time, would the product still be better than without it? If not, I either reduce the error rate with grounding, constraints and review, or I do not build it. Often the answer is hybrid: a rule for the decision and a model for the wording.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c25-01\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C25-01</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> ai · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C25 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AI · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> What is an eval, and why would you build it before the feature?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;It's a test of the AI.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Describes a test set, a rubric and a target, and says it guides building.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Explains the eval as the specification, covers real and adversarial cases, slices, human and model judges, regression runs on every change, and the hidden test set.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> An eval is a repeatable test: a set of cases, a way to score the outputs, and a target. For an AI feature it is the specification, because I cannot write &quot;the answer shall be correct&quot; as a rule; I can only say what good looks like on concrete cases. I build it first for three reasons. It decides what I build: writing down the failures that matter (an invented policy, a missed urgent message, a leak of another person's data) shapes the design. It lets me compare approaches fairly: prompt against retrieval, one model against another, scored on the same cases. And it makes change safe: every prompt, model or data change reruns the eval, so a fix in one place does not quietly break another. What goes in it: real inputs from the intended use, including the awkward ones, other languages, adversarial inputs and questions with no answer; a short rubric with scored examples; judges (people first, rules where checkable, a calibrated model grader to extend reach); a pass bar tied to the cost of an error; and per-slice results, because an average can hide a failing category. I keep part of the set hidden from the engineers who tune the system, and I add every production failure to a &quot;never again&quot; set.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c25-02\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C25-02</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> ai · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C25 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AI · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> How many examples do you need in an evaluation set?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;A few hundred should be fine.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Links the number to the precision needed and the number of slices.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Gives and applies the formula, notes that small sets cannot certify a low error rate, explains the rule of three, and plans per-slice samples and a floor.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> It depends on how precisely I need to know an error rate and how many groups I want to read separately. To estimate a rate p within ±δ at 95% confidence, I need about n = 1.96² × p × (1 − p) ÷ δ² cases. For an expected error of 2% within ±1 point, n is about 753. For 5% within ±2 points, about 456. For 3% within ±1.5 points, about 497. <em>Slices multiply the need.</em> If I want to read ten languages separately at that precision, I need about ten times as many cases: about 7,500 for the 2% example. When that is too expensive, I set a smaller floor per slice (100 cases reads a 3% rate only to about ±3.3 points) and use it as a gross-failure gate, and put more cases where traffic and risk are highest. <em>Small sets and zero errors.</em> Thirty cases with no errors do not show that the error rate is low; the 95% upper bound is still about 3 ÷ 30 = 10% (the rule of three). To claim a rate below 1% from a clean run needs about 300 cases. <em>Quality matters more than count.</em> Five hundred cases from the real distribution beat 5,000 cases that are all easy. Check that the set represents real use, including rare, costly situations, and refresh it.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c25-03\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C25-03</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> ai · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C25 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AI · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Apple</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> How would you evaluate the quality and safety of on-device summarization of notifications?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Test it with some users and see if they like it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Defines quality and safety criteria, builds a test set, and notes the privacy limit on data.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Handles the constraint that raw user notifications cannot be collected, sizes samples by language, covers sensitive categories and urgent signals, uses calibrated model graders and privacy-preserving measurement, and plans shadow mode, rollout and monitoring.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general; nothing here describes any real feature or process <code>[as of 2026-10]</code>. The central difficulty is privacy: the point of the feature is that notifications stay on the device, so I cannot simply collect users' real notifications to test it. The eval design has to work around that. <em>What good means (FW-C25-01).</em> Failures: an unsupported claim (a summary says something no notification said), a dropped urgent signal (&quot;call me now&quot; lost), a wrong sender or attribution, a summary of something that should never be summarized (a verification code, a medical or emergency alert), and poor quality in some languages. <em>Where the cases come from.</em> (1) Synthetic notifications written to cover app types, languages and styles, and labeled as synthetic. (2) Notifications donated by consenting testers (employees, paid volunteers) under clear terms. (3) Public or licensed message corpora where appropriate. (4) Aggregated, privacy-preserving on-device measurement after launch, such as counts of &quot;show original&quot; taps, opt-outs and a rate of locally computed quality checks reported without content <code>[unverified: author's design suggestion; no source checked]</code>. Keep the cases diverse: short and long, multi-sender, slang, mixed languages, urgent and trivial. <em>Sizing.</em> To read a 2% unsupported-claim rate within ±1 point in one language needs about 753 cases (1.96² × 0.02 × 0.98 ÷ 0.01²); for ten languages, about 7,530 summaries. At two reviewers each at $0.40 a label, labeling costs 7,530 × 2 × $0.40 = about $6,000, small relative to the risk. For lower-traffic languages use a smaller floor and restrict launch to languages that pass. <em>Judges.</em> Human reviewers with language fluency score a sample against the rubric; I check their agreement beyond chance and fix the rubric until it is reliable. A model grader screens the rest after calibration against the humans, with a human sample of what it passes, since it will miss some failures. <em>Safety tests.</em> A targeted set of sensitive notifications (security codes, health alerts, emergency messages, content involving minors): the pass bar is that the rules route all of them to &quot;show original&quot; with zero summarized in the test, and the upper bound on the miss rate for the set size is stated (for 500 cases with no misses, about 0.6%). Adversarial tests: notifications containing instructions to the model. <em>Pass bar and rollout.</em> At most 2% unsupported claims and 1% dropped urgent signals per language, zero summaries of the excluded categories in testing. Run in shadow mode on devices of consenting testers, then a small public group, with the metrics above and a kill switch. Re-evaluate on every model or prompt update. <em>Company note.</em> Apple is the setting only: <code>Public</code> that Apple has described on-device processing and privacy as a design principle, <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports of evaluation-under-privacy prompts <code>[V: Apple privacy pages; the prompt descriptions are candidate-reported and unverified]</code>. No claim is made about any real feature or process.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c25-04\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C25-04</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> ai · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C25 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AI · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Microsoft</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Design the evaluation for an assistant that answers employees' questions from company documents.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Ask employees if the answers are good.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Builds a question set and evaluates retrieval, correctness and abstention.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates retrieval, groundedness, correctness and abstention; includes permission tests and unanswerable questions; sets release gates with sample-size reasoning; and monitors drift as documents change.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general; nothing here describes any real product. I'd evaluate the pipeline in parts and end to end. <em>The set.</em> About 600 real questions from search logs and help-desk tickets, stratified by department (HR, IT, finance, legal), with a fifth deliberately unanswerable from the documents, and a few dozen adversarial ones (instructions hidden in a question, requests for documents the asker may not see). Domain owners write the reference answer and the source passage for each. <em>Measures.</em> <em>Retrieval:</em> is the right passage in the top four? <em>Groundedness:</em> is every claim supported by the cited passage? <em>Correctness:</em> does the answer match the reference? <em>Abstention:</em> does it say &quot;not found&quot; on the unanswerable? <em>Citation validity:</em> do the cited sources exist and support the claim? <em>Permission safety:</em> with test accounts of different access, no content from documents they may not read, tested on every change. <em>Sizing.</em> To read a confident-wrong rate near 5% within ±2.5 points needs about 292 cases (1.96² × 0.05 × 0.95 ÷ 0.025²), so 600 total supports the overall reading, with a floor of about 100 per department for gross failures and more in the sensitive ones (HR, legal). <em>Judges.</em> Domain experts score a sample; two scorers check agreement; a model grader screens the rest after calibration with a human sample of what it passes. <em>Release gate.</em> For example: confident-wrong at most 5% overall and at most 2% in HR, legal and finance; citation validity at least 95%; zero permission leaks in the test; abstention on at least 85% of unanswerable questions; per-department floors. The gate runs on every change to the model, prompt, retrieval or index. <em>Production.</em> Review 100 sampled answers a week; track &quot;not helpful&quot; reports, escalations and the age of cited documents; watch drift as documents change (an old policy gives a confident wrong answer), and add failures to the &quot;never again&quot; set. Launch department by department, starting where documents are cleanest. <em>Company note.</em> Microsoft is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports of enterprise-assistant evaluation prompts <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Microsoft data or design is claimed.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c25-05\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C25-05</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> ai · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C25 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AI · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Google</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Can we use a language model to grade the answers of another language model?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Yes, it saves money,&quot; or &quot;no, it can't be trusted.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Says to calibrate it against human judgments.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Measures the judge's precision and recall on real failures, explains why accuracy misleads with rare failures, names its biases, and designs a triage with human samples.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general; nothing here describes any real system. Yes, with care: it is a useful tool and a poor final authority until measured. <em>Calibrate first.</em> Take a sample of outputs judged by trained people, then have the model judge the same outputs against the same rubric, and compare. Do not stop at agreement. Suppose, of 1,000 outputs, humans find 100 bad. The model flags 80 as bad, 70 of them right and 10 wrong. Precision is 70 ÷ 80 = 87.5%; recall is 70 ÷ 100 = 70%; overall accuracy is (70 + 890) ÷ 1,000 = 96%. The 96% sounds excellent and hides that the judge lets 30% of the bad outputs through. <em>Known weaknesses.</em> Preference for longer or more fluent answers; trouble verifying facts it was not given; sensitivity to the order of answers shown to it; leniency toward outputs that resemble its own style; mistakes correlated with the system being graded. <em>Make it better.</em> Give it the source documents and ask claim-by-claim checks; use a separate check per rubric criterion instead of one overall score; include examples of subtle failures in its instructions; use a different model family from the one being graded where possible; randomize answer order. Recalibrate after each change. <em>Use it as triage.</em> Run it on everything, send the flagged items to humans along with a random sample of the passed ones to estimate the miss rate, and track that miss rate over time. Do not gate a launch on the judge alone for high-stakes use. <em>Company note.</em> Google is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports of generated-answer evaluation prompts <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Google data or practice is claimed.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c25-06\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C25-06</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> ai · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C25 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AI · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Meta</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Suggested replies in a messaging product are accepted 38% of the time. Is the feature working?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Yes, 38% is good adoption.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Questions whether acceptance measures quality and asks about errors.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Measures accepted-and-wrong, computes the break-even error rate from time saved and cost of error, reads effects by week, checks sensitive contexts and long-term effects with a holdout, and sets a launch rule.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general; nothing here describes any real product. Acceptance tells me people tapped the suggestion; it does not tell me the suggestion was right, appropriate or helpful. It could reflect a useful feature or one users trust too much. <em>Check what was accepted.</em> Sample accepted suggestions and have reviewers judge them: wrong facts, wrong tone for the relationship, inappropriate for the moment. Suppose 5% are bad. Track later corrections, follow-up messages and complaints too. <em>Break-even.</em> Suppose an accepted suggestion saves 18 seconds and a bad one costs the user 6 minutes (360 seconds) in repair or embarrassment. The break-even error rate is 18 ÷ 360 = 5%, since the saving applies to every acceptance and each bad one adds 360 seconds of repair. At 5% bad among accepted the feature is a wash for the user; below it helps, above it hurts. So 38% acceptance with 5% bad is no evidence of value, and I would want the error rate well below 5% with an interval below the break-even. <em>Time and learning.</em> Read acceptance by week: early novelty inflates it, and users learn which suggestions to trust, so it may fall. Compare with a holdout over several weeks on messaging volume, satisfaction, and relationship-sensitive outcomes (conversations that end abruptly after a suggested reply). <em>Context risk.</em> Replies in sensitive contexts (condolences, conflict, health, money) cost more when wrong; exclude or restrict suggestions there, and measure by context. <em>Launch rule.</em> Accepted-and-wrong at most 2% overall and near zero in sensitive contexts; complaints no higher than the holdout; no drop in conversation health measures; and monitoring after launch. What would change my view: evidence that bad suggestions concentrate in a segment or topic, which I can remove while keeping the benefit elsewhere. <em>Company note.</em> Meta is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports of feature-experiment prompts <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Meta data or practice is claimed.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c25-07\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C25-07</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> ai · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C25 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AI · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Amazon</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Price an AI assistant add-on for sellers on a marketplace so that it makes money.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Charge a flat monthly fee similar to competitors.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Computes cost per task and margin and considers heavy users.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Computes margin by segment, shows how an average hides losses, designs allowance and overage, routing and caps, tests price elasticity, and ties price to seller value.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general; nothing here describes any real product. A flat fee is attractive to sellers and risky because AI cost scales with use. <em>Cost by segment.</em> Suppose a seller's average use is 150 tasks a month at $0.04 each ($6) plus $1.50 of other cost: $7.50. A heavy seller (10% of sellers) runs 900 tasks: $36 plus $1.50: $37.50. At a flat $15 a month: average margin $7.50 (50%), heavy margin −$22.50. Blended cost: 0.9 × $7.50 + 0.1 × $37.50 = $10.50, so the margin is $4.50, 30%. <em>Redesign.</em> Include 400 tasks a month and charge $0.05 per additional task. The heavy seller then pays $15 + 500 × $0.05 = $40 against a cost of $37.50: a small positive margin. Blended revenue: 0.9 × $15 + 0.1 × $40 = $17.50, margin $17.50 − $10.50 = $7.00, 40%. Average sellers see no change. <em>Other levers.</em> Route simple tasks to cheaper models (cost per task falls; test quality by route); cache repeated outputs; shorten prompts; offer a higher-priced tier for power users; consider pricing by outcome where value is measurable (a listing that sells). <em>Value anchor.</em> Price relative to what the seller gains: if the assistant saves a seller five hours a month or lifts conversion, a $15 fee is small; measure that benefit and use it in the message. <em>Test.</em> Price experiments by cohort for elasticity (conversion, churn, usage), watch complaints about overage, and monitor margin by cohort monthly. Keep in mind the marketplace context: sellers who feel nickel-and-dimed may leave, so make allowances generous enough that most never see overage. <em>Company note.</em> Amazon is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports of pricing and unit-economics prompts <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Amazon data or pricing is claimed.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c25-08\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C25-08</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> ai · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C25 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AI · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Stripe</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> An AI agent handles payment disputes and refunds for support. How do you decide how much autonomy to give it, and when to give it more?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Let it handle small refunds on its own.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Sets approval levels by amount and error cost.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Defines promotion evidence with sample sizes, per-band limits, demotion triggers, audit and reversibility, and weighs the economics against the cost of error.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general; nothing here describes any real system. Autonomy should be earned per action and per amount band, with evidence and a way back (FW-C25-05). <em>Start low.</em> At first the agent drafts the decision and the reply; a person approves each one. This gathers data on how often the agent is right, for each type and amount. <em>Promotion evidence.</em> To promote small refunds (under $20) to autonomous, require: at least 300 supervised cases in that band with no errors (the upper 95% bound on the error rate is then about 1%, by the rule of three), or about 600 for a 0.5% bound; no severe incident; low override rate by reviewers; reversibility (a refund can be reversed or limited); and monitoring and a kill switch in place. Each amount band and each case type (duplicate charge, item not received, fraud claim) earns its own evidence; reliability on $10 duplicate charges says little about $500 fraud disputes. <em>Limits that bound the risk.</em> Caps on amount per refund and per customer per month, a daily total, a rule that suspicious patterns go to a person, an audit log of every action with the evidence used, and a short reversal window. <em>Demotion.</em> A written rule: if the error rate in a week exceeds 1% on at least 100 cases, or any severe incident occurs (a refund to the wrong party, a fraud loss above a set amount), drop that band back to approval and investigate. <em>Economics.</em> Suppose 50,000 refunds a month, and automating a refund saves $2 of handling: $100,000 a month. If 0.5% are wrong at an average cost of $30, errors cost 250 × $30 = $7,500 a month, a small fraction of the savings, so promotion pays when the evidence supports a 0.5% bound. For larger amounts the cost of an error rises with the amount, and the evidence bar rises with it. <em>Fraud and abuse.</em> Refund agents attract manipulation (a customer's message instructing the agent); treat message text as untrusted, never let it change limits, and require extra checks for risky signals. <em>Company note.</em> Stripe is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports of automation-with-money prompts <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Stripe data or practice is claimed.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c25-09\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C25-09</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> ai · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C25 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AI · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Your company sells an AI product in several regions. How do you approach regulation and compliance?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Ask legal to check it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Classifies by risk and notes regional differences.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Triages each use by risk tier and market, uses current dates carefully (including deferrals), builds documentation and transparency into the product, and sets owners and launch implications, without giving legal advice.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I am not a lawyer and rules change quickly, so I'd involve legal and policy from the start and check current texts <code>[as of 2026-10]</code>. My job as PM is to give them a clear picture and build the product to meet what they decide. <em>Triage (FW-C25-07).</em> List each AI use and each market. Classify by risk tier: prohibited uses; high-risk uses (employment decisions, credit, education, essential services, safety components); limited-risk uses with transparency duties (chatbots must disclose that the user is dealing with a machine; generated content may need labeling); minimal risk (a spam filter). Take an example: a customer-service chatbot is typically limited-risk, so the main duty is disclosure; a resume-screening feature is typically high-risk, with obligations on documentation, human oversight, data quality, and testing for bias. <em>Know the dates, and read deferrals carefully.</em> In the European Union, for example, prohibited practices have applied since February 2025, obligations for general-purpose model providers since August 2025, and transparency duties for chatbots and generated content from 2 August 2026 (with a transition to 2 December 2026 for machine-readable marking by generative systems already on the market); the obligations for high-risk systems, originally due 2 August 2026, were deferred by Regulation (EU) 2026/1744 to 2 December 2027 for stand-alone systems and 2 August 2028 for AI embedded in regulated products <code>[V: Regulation (EU) 2026/1744 on EUR-Lex; legal summaries for the dates]</code>. A deferral is not an exemption: the work of documentation and testing takes time, and transparency duties may already apply. <em>Build in what is required.</em> Disclosure that the user is interacting with an AI; labeling of generated content; human review for significant decisions; records of the system's purpose, data, evaluation results, known limits and monitoring (a model card or equivalent); logs for audit; data protection (what is collected, retention, user rights). <em>Other regions.</em> In the United States, existing consumer-protection and anti-discrimination laws apply and some states and cities have AI-specific rules <code>[V: US: April 2023 joint statement by EEOC, CFPB, FTC and DOJ on existing law; NYC Local Law 144; other state rules not enumerated]</code>; in India, data protection law, non-binding AI governance guidelines and binding IT Rules amendments on synthetic content apply <code>[V: India: non-binding MeitY guidelines and binding IT Rules amendments, per law-firm summaries, 2026]</code>; in China, specific rules cover recommendation algorithms and generative services <code>[V: China: algorithm, deep synthesis and generative AI rules, per law-firm summaries]</code>. A feature may need different designs, or a delayed launch, in different markets. <em>Operate it.</em> Assign owners (legal, policy, security, product), put compliance gates into the launch checklist, and review when rules change.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c25-10\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C25-10</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> ai · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C25 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AI · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> What is the moat of an AI writing assistant if the underlying models keep improving?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Our AI is better than the competition's.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names data, distribution or workflow as sources of advantage.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Tests each source for durability, separates what compounds from what does not, notes that model access is not a moat, and says what to build toward.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd be honest: the model itself is unlikely to be the moat if others can access the same or better models. So I'd test each candidate source with the sources-of-advantage test (FW-C18-05). <em>Weak sources.</em> A clever prompt (easy to copy and to replace); a thin wrapper around a general model; the choice of model (rivals can switch); novelty. <em>Possible sources.</em> (1) <em>Distribution:</em> if the assistant sits inside the tools where people already write (email, documents), it reaches users that a standalone rival must win. (2) <em>Workflow integration and switching costs:</em> the more it holds a person's or team's templates, style guides, past documents and approvals, the harder it is to leave. (3) <em>Proprietary data and feedback loops:</em> data that others lack and that improves with use (what edits users make to suggestions, house style) can compound, though a stronger general model may erase the advantage if the task does not need your data. (4) <em>Evaluation capability:</em> a mature test set and monitoring let me improve faster and ship safely; a new entrant starts from nothing. (5) <em>Trust and accountability:</em> in domains with costly errors (legal, medical, finance), a record of reliability, audit trails and clear responsibility matters. (6) <em>Cost position:</em> routing and caching deliver the same quality for less, which allows a better price or margin. <em>What compounds.</em> Workflow depth, data from use and trust compound; prompts and model choice do not. So I'd invest in becoming part of how teams write: shared style guides, review flows, integrations, and an eval that makes each model upgrade safe to adopt. <em>What I'd watch.</em> Whether competitors with the same models match our quality; whether the platforms we depend on add the feature natively; and whether our retention comes from habit and integration or from novelty.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c26-01\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C26-01</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product-sense · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C26 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> How do you decide whether a new idea is worth pursuing?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I'd check whether the market is big and the idea is good.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Starts from the problem and uses criteria.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Scores with evidence quality, names the assumptions behind the score, compares alternatives and decides what to test first.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;I start with the problem, not the idea: who has it, how often, how painful it is, what they do today and what they spend on it. Then I score the idea on a few criteria: severity and frequency, how many people have it, willingness to pay or switch, our advantage in doing it, feasibility, differentiation and fit with strategy. For each score I note the evidence: strong if I have observed behavior or a payment, medium if from interviews or surveys, weak if it's opinion or analogy, and I discount the weak ones. That gives a ranking by what I know, not by what I hope. &quot;The scorecard doesn't decide; it tells me what to test. I list the assumptions behind the top ideas, find the one that would sink the idea if it were false and about which I know the least, usually that people will pay, and design the cheapest test that could prove it wrong. I decide in advance what result would make me continue, stop or change direction.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Problem first; criteria with evidence quality; assumptions; a test with pre-set rules.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c26-02\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C26-02</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product-sense · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C26 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> What is a minimum viable product, and what is it not?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;The first version with the basic features.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> The smallest thing that tests an assumption.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Links the MVP to a specific hypothesis, gives forms that are not software, says what it is not (a poor product, a small launch), and names a success rule.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;An MVP is the smallest thing you can build or do that lets you test the riskiest assumption about an idea and learn from real behavior. It is defined by the question it answers, not by how many features it has. So it may not be software at all: a landing page with a price and a deposit tests demand; a video shows whether people understand and want it; a concierge version, where you do the service by hand for a few customers, tests value and economics; a Wizard-of-Oz version looks automated and is staffed behind the scenes; a prototype tests usability. &quot;What it is not: a bad product shipped because time ran out; a small launch with no hypothesis; or a demo that cannot fail. A good MVP has a hypothesis ('students will put down a $5 deposit for a $40-a-week meal kit'), a measure, a threshold set before the test and a decision. If the test can't change your mind, it isn't an MVP; it's a demonstration.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> The question defines it; non-software forms; what it is not; a threshold.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c26-03\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C26-03</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product-sense · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C26 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> You have an idea for a service that matches volunteers with local charities that need help for a few hours. How would you validate it cheaply?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Build an app and see if people sign up.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names assumptions and a cheap test.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Ranks assumptions, designs a test with measure, threshold and sample size, uses a concierge stage, and handles both sides of the marketplace.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;It's a two-sided idea, so I'd list assumptions for each side. Volunteers: they want short, flexible opportunities and will show up (value; uncertain). Charities: they have small tasks, can describe them, and will use a new channel (value; uncertain). Viability: matching by hand can be made cheap enough, and there is a way to fund it. The riskiest is probably whether charities will post tasks and whether volunteers will show up for them, since a marketplace with no supply or no demand is nothing (C19). &quot;Cheapest test: a concierge version. I'd contact 15 charities, ask for a list of tasks they can't staff, and match volunteers by hand through a messaging group of 100 people from one neighborhood. Measure: tasks posted per charity, the share of tasks filled within a week and the show-up rate. Thresholds, set in advance: proceed if at least 10 of 15 charities post a task and at least 60% of tasks are filled with 80% show-up; stop if fewer than 5 charities post. For a landing-page test on the volunteer side, to estimate a sign-up rate near 5% within ±2 points I'd need about 450 visitors (1.96² × 0.05 × 0.95 ÷ 0.02²). Then I'd look at what the manual work costs per match, to see what must be automated, and keep the geography small so that both sides find each other.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Both sides; the riskiest assumption; a concierge test with thresholds; economics.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c26-04\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C26-04</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product-sense · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C26 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> How do you tell whether a product has product-market fit?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;When lots of people use it and it's growing.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names retention and a disappointment survey.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Reads the retention curve shape, the survey by segment, organic growth and willingness to pay, and distinguishes false signals (waiting lists, launch spikes, paid dependence, incentives).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;I look for a pattern, not one number. First, retention: plot cohorts and see whether the curve flattens at a non-zero level. If it levels at, say, 25%, a core of users keeps coming back; if it falls to zero, there's no fit. Second, a survey asking how users would feel if they could no longer use the product, with 'very disappointed' as the strongest answer; a commonly cited threshold is about 40% <code>[V: Sean Ellis survey and 40% benchmark, as described in Rahul Vohra's First Round article]</code>, and I'd read it by segment, since an average can hide a group with strong fit. Third, organic growth: users arriving and inviting others without being paid. Fourth, usage frequency in the target segment and willingness to pay. Fifth, qualitative pull: customers complaining when it breaks. &quot;False signals: a waiting list or a launch spike (interest, not commitment); growth that stops when ad spend stops; usage driven by incentives; praise from friends; and averages that hide that one segment loves the product and the rest don't. The question I ask is: what happens to the numbers if marketing stops?&quot; <em>Signals.</em> A pattern of signals; segment reading; false signals.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c26-05\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C26-05</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product-sense · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C26 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Your landing-page smoke test got a 5% sign-up rate. Is that good?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Yes, 5% is good.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Asks about the threshold, the audience and the sample.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Computes the interval, compares with a pre-set threshold, considers the commitment level, the traffic source and the price, and decides the next test.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;I can't say without three things: the threshold we set before the test, the size of the sample and who the visitors were. With 300 visitors, 5% (15 sign-ups) has a standard error of about 1.3 points, so the 95% interval is roughly 2.5% to 7.5%: it could be well below a 4% bar. With 800 visitors it narrows to about ±1.5 points. So first the interval. &quot;Second, the commitment: an email sign-up is the weakest signal; a deposit or pre-order is stronger. If the page showed a price and 5% still signed up, it is more meaningful than if no price was shown. Third, the audience: visitors from a community of enthusiasts or from friends will convert more than the target segment; I'd look at conversion by source, and at who the sign-ups are. Fourth, what we compare with: we have no universal benchmark; compare with our threshold, with a control page, or with similar offers we've run. &quot;If the threshold was 4% and 1,200 visitors gave an interval of about 3.8% to 6.2%, the lower end sits just under the bar, so I'd extend the test before calling it a pass. If the whole interval were above 4%, that would be a pass on sign-ups, and I'd then test a stronger commitment: a deposit, or a concierge pilot with paying customers.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> The interval; threshold, source and commitment; the next test.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c26-06\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C26-06</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product-sense · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C26 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A team has worked on a new product for six months and the key metrics are flat. Should it kill, persist or pivot?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Give it more time,&quot; or &quot;kill it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Checks the criteria and the evidence.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Uses criteria set in advance, reads by segment and cohort, applies the decision table, checks sunk cost, designs the next test and a time box, and handles the team's attachment.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;I'd start with the criteria the team set at the beginning: what thresholds on retention or the disappointment survey would have meant stop? If none were set, I'd write them now, before looking at more data, to avoid moving the goalposts. Then I'd read the data properly: cohort retention (flat overall may hide a segment that retains), the survey by segment, and who the retained users are. &quot;Then the decision table. Weak everywhere on tests that were well designed means kill; strong in a narrow segment means pivot to that segment; inconclusive because the test was poor means persist with a better test. Suppose retention flattens at 11% and one segment is 46% very disappointed against 18% elsewhere: that is a pivot, treated as a hypothesis with new kill criteria, say retention of 20% in that segment at week eight, and a time box of eight weeks. I'd also name sunk cost: the six months are gone either way and shouldn't weigh. &quot;For the team: I'd separate the idea from the people, thank them for what was learned, and make sure that stopping is seen as the result of a good test, not a failure.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Pre-set criteria; segment reading; the decision table; sunk cost; a time box; team care.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c26-07\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C26-07</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product-sense · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C26 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> You are asked to build a product for smallholder farmers in a region where there is little data. What do you build first?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;An app that connects farmers to buyers.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Starts with research on the users and the problem.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Researches the farmers' actual jobs and constraints (connectivity, trust, payments, language), identifies the riskiest assumption, runs an assisted concierge test through channels they already use, measures real transactions, and plans how to scale.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;I'd begin with the farmers, not the app: observe and interview 20 to 30 farmers about how they sell today, to whom, at what price, how they get paid, what goes wrong and what they would pay or give up to fix it. The constraints matter: patchy connectivity, low-end phones, limited literacy in some languages, trust in middlemen and in digital payments (C09). &quot;Assumptions: farmers want a different way to sell (value); they'll use a channel they already know (usability: voice or a messaging app, not a new app); buyers will pay enough for reliable supply (viability); logistics and payment can be handled (feasibility). The riskiest is probably trust and the transaction itself: will a farmer sell through us and will the buyer pay? &quot;First build: a concierge marketplace through a phone line and a messaging group. We take produce listings from 40 farmers, match them with 6 buyers by hand, and handle payment through a method both use. Measure: listings per farmer, the share of listings sold, price compared with the farmer's usual price, repeat sales in four weeks. Thresholds: proceed if 30% of farmers (12 of 40) sell at least twice and the average price is at least 10% better than the middleman price. The learning is what to automate: matching, price information, payment. I'd decide the app only after the manual process works.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Research first; local constraints; riskiest assumption; a concierge test with thresholds.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c26-08\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C26-08</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product-sense · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C26 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> You have two zero-to-one ideas and little data on either. How do you decide which to pursue?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Pick the bigger market.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Compares on criteria and effort.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Uses evidence-adjusted scoring and a simple expected-value comparison including the cost and speed of testing, prefers the idea with faster learning when values are close, and considers reversibility and learning value.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;I'd compare them on the criteria with evidence quality, then on expected value and learning speed. Suppose idea X has a 30% chance of working and a payoff of $5 million, and idea Y a 15% chance and a payoff of $12 million (all guesses, so I'd give ranges). Expected value: X is 0.30 × $5M = $1.5M; Y is 0.15 × $12M = $1.8M. After test costs of $20,000 and $30,000: $1.48M and $1.77M. On paper Y wins, but the numbers are weak guesses. &quot;So I'd add what the guesses don't capture. Learning speed: if I can test X in three weeks and Y in eight, I can resolve X sooner and move on if it fails, which has value. Evidence quality: if the success probability for Y rests on an analogy, I'd discount it. Reversibility and learning: which test also teaches something useful for the other idea or for the company? If the values are close and one test is much faster, I'd run the faster test first and keep the other in the queue; or, if the tests are cheap, run both small tests in parallel for a few weeks and decide on the results, which is often better than debating guesses.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Evidence-adjusted comparison; expected value with caution; learning speed; parallel small tests.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c27-01\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C27-01</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy-gtm · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C27 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> What makes a marketplace hard to start, and what is liquidity?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;You need both buyers and sellers.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Explains the cold start and defines liquidity.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Measures liquidity with availability, conversion and utilization, finds the binding side, and describes a start in one narrow market.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;A marketplace is worth something only if both sides are there, and each waits for the other: sellers won't list with no buyers, buyers won't come with nothing to buy. Liquidity is how likely a participant is to find a good match quickly. I measure it with the share of searches that show at least one available, relevant result, conversion once a result is shown, and how much of suppliers' capacity is used. The booking rate is the product of the first two. &quot;To start, I'd pick one narrow market (a neighborhood, a category) and make it work there before expanding. I'd find which side is short: if 60% of searches end with nothing available, supply is short; if hosts' utilization is a fifth of normal, demand is short. Then I'd add the short side in steps, checking that the other side isn't stranded.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> The cold start; measured liquidity; the binding side; a narrow start.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c27-02\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C27-02</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy-gtm · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C27 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Microsoft</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Who are the buyer, the user and the administrator of an enterprise product, and why does it matter?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;The customer is the company and the users are employees.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Distinguishes the buyer, user and administrator and what each needs.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Adds security, procurement and champions, explains the sales cycle and non-feature requirements, and says how this changes the roadmap.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general; nothing here describes any real product or company. &quot;The economic buyer holds the budget and cares about cost, risk and results; the users do the work in the product and care about speed and ease; the administrators configure, secure and support it and care about control, audit and reliability. Add a champion who wants it, IT and security who can block it, and procurement and legal who set terms. Together they are the buying committee, which makes the sales cycle long: months of evaluations, reviews and approvals. &quot;It matters because a product users love can still stall if it lacks single sign-on, access control, audit logs, data controls or compliance certifications, which decide deals though they are not features users ask for. So the roadmap has three streams: user value, administrator control and enterprise requirements, and the PM balances them. I'd also design for expansion: land with a team, then grow within the account, and track net revenue retention.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> The actors; non-feature requirements; the cycle; roadmap consequence.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c27-03\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C27-03</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy-gtm · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C27 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Amazon</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> How would you reduce counterfeit and low-quality sellers in a third-party marketplace without hurting good sellers?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Verify sellers and remove bad ones.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Layers prevention, detection and enforcement, and notes false positives.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Measures prevalence by sampling, sets thresholds by error cost, uses brand and buyer reports, designs appeals, and watches adaptation and fairness.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general; I describe a method and claim nothing about any real company's programs <code>Inferred</code>. &quot;First measure the problem: sample 5,000 listings for expert review and find, say, 40 counterfeit, a prevalence of 0.8% with a 95% interval of about ±0.25 points; across 2 million listings that is about 16,000. Then layers. <em>Prevention:</em> verify identity and business details at onboarding, with stricter checks in high-risk categories; let brands register and gate sensitive categories. <em>Detection:</em> models on listing text, images, price and seller behavior, plus reports from buyers and brands. <em>Enforcement:</em> graduated actions (warn, limit, suspend), with appeals. <em>Incentives:</em> make counterfeits unprofitable (holds on payouts for new sellers in risky categories). &quot;Tradeoffs: stricter checks slow good new sellers and block honest ones, so I'd set thresholds by the cost of each error, use friction (extra verification) for the borderline band, and measure appeal overturn rates. Measures: prevalence (sampled), buyer complaints per 1,000 orders, time to remove, seller onboarding completion and false-suspension rate. Adversaries adapt, so I'd resample monthly and red-team the detection.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Sampled prevalence; layers; error-cost thresholds; appeals; adaptation.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c27-04\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C27-04</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy-gtm · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C27 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Stripe</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> How would you reduce chargebacks on a payments product without hurting conversion?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Block more risky payments.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Separates fraud from other disputes and compares costs.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Segments chargebacks by cause, uses friction for a borderline band, computes net benefit with the cost of lost conversion, and monitors false declines.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general; I describe a method and no real product's numbers <code>Inferred</code>. &quot;First understand the chargebacks: 2 million payments a month at an average $60 with a 0.45% chargeback rate is 9,000 chargebacks, and with the $15 fee about $675,000 a month. Split by cause: true fraud, 'friendly fraud' (the cardholder disputes a purchase they made), merchandise problems (not received, not as described), and unclear billing descriptors. The fixes differ: fraud needs screening; friendly fraud needs evidence and clear receipts; merchandise problems need merchant quality; descriptors need clarity. &quot;For fraud, blocking more costs good customers, so I'd add friction only for a risky band. Suppose 100,000 payments a month are in the borderline band with 4,500 of the chargebacks, and an extra verification step cuts those by 40% (1,800 × $75 = $135,000 saved) but loses 3% of the band's payments (3,000 × ($1.80 margin + $6 future value) = $23,400). Net about $111,600 a month. I'd test it on a holdout and measure conversion, chargebacks and false declines. For the other causes: clearer descriptors, receipts and delivery confirmation, and a dispute-evidence tool for merchants.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Chargebacks by cause; a friction band; net benefit with lost conversion; monitoring.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c27-05\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C27-05</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy-gtm · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C27 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> How does an ad auction decide which ad to show and what it costs?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;The highest bidder wins.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Explains that predicted response is combined with the bid.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Works a numeric example with scores and the winner's price, explains why weighting protects users and revenue, and names calibration and ad-load guardrails.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;Each time a slot opens, the system scores each eligible ad by its bid times the predicted chance the user will respond, which is the expected revenue per impression, and shows the highest score. The winner pays the least that would keep its position, close to what the next competitor needed. Example: A bids $2.00 with a 1.0% predicted click rate (score 0.020), B bids $3.00 with 0.5% (0.015), C bids $1.50 with 1.2% (0.018). A wins. Its price per click is C's score divided by A's rate: 0.018 ÷ 0.010 = $1.80, not $2.00. B's higher bid loses because users are unlikely to click. &quot;Weighting by predicted response raises revenue (A earns $18 per thousand impressions at its price, against $15 for B at its bid), protects users from irrelevant ads and rewards good ads. It depends on calibrated predictions; if they're wrong, revenue and quality fall. Guardrails: ad load, retention and complaints.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Expected value ranking; the price rule; a numeric example; guardrails.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c27-06\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C27-06</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy-gtm · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C27 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Design the trust and safety approach for a new live-video feature.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Add a report button and moderators.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names harms and layers.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Starts from harms and adversaries, designs prevention by design (limits, age and account requirements), real-time detection, human review, appeals and measures prevalence, and plans a staged rollout with a kill switch.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;Live video is hard because harm happens in real time and cannot be reviewed first. I'd start with the harms (violence, sexual content, harassment, scams, self-harm) and the adversaries who will adapt. Layers: <em>prevention by design:</em> require account age and verification to broadcast, start with small audiences, limit features for new accounts, delay or sample streams from high-risk accounts; <em>detection:</em> automated checks on audio, video and chat, with a lower threshold for severe harms; <em>response:</em> the ability to interrupt a stream quickly, human review of flagged streams within minutes, and a kill switch per feature; <em>after:</em> appeals and penalties. &quot;Measures: prevalence of violating live views by sampling, time to interrupt, proactive detection rate, appeal overturn rate and legitimate-creator friction. Tradeoffs: a lower threshold stops more harm and interrupts more good streams; I'd set it by harm severity. Rollout: invite-only, then a small share of regions, with review of incidents and red-team tests before expanding.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Harms first; layers designed for real time; prevalence; staged rollout.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c27-07\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C27-07</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy-gtm · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C27 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> You are the PM of an internal platform used by 40 teams. Only 12 have adopted it. What do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Mandate that teams use it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Finds out why teams haven't adopted.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Segments the non-adopters, measures time to first use and the cost of migration, fixes the biggest barrier, uses early adopters as proof, and considers mandates only with evidence and a transition plan.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;Adoption is 30% (12 of 40). I'd interview a sample of non-adopters and look at why: it doesn't fit their use case, migration is too costly, it's less reliable than their own solution, they don't know about it, or they distrust the team's support. Then quantify: time to first successful use, migration effort, and the value they'd get (time saved, incidents avoided). &quot;Fix the largest barrier first: if migration takes three weeks and the benefit is small, build migration tools or support; if it doesn't fit a use case shared by ten teams, add it; if reliability is the issue, publish and meet service levels. Use the 12 adopters as proof: collect results (hours saved) and have them help others. A mandate is a last resort: it produces compliance and resentment, and works only when the benefit is clear and a transition is funded. Measure adoption by workload moved, not by teams signed, with guardrails on reliability and customer satisfaction. I'd also be willing to say no to the teams whose needs are too different, and say so.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Diagnosis of non-adoption; barrier-first; proof; careful use of mandates.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c27-08\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C27-08</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy-gtm · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C27 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> As a growth PM you raised weekly active users by 12%, but 90-day retention fell. What do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Keep the growth; retention often drops with more users.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Segments new users and checks quality.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates mix shift from a real change in retention, reads cohorts by acquisition source and tactic, computes the net effect on long-term active users and acquisition payback, checks for dark patterns, and decides by a rule.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;First check whether it's mix or real. If the growth came from a new channel or tactic whose users retain less, overall retention falls even if nothing else changed; compare retention by cohort and source. Then compute the net effect on long-term active users: say a baseline of 100,000 new users with 30% retained at 90 days is 30,000 retained; the growth tactic adds 12,000 new users with 10% retention (1,200 retained) while lowering retention among existing sources by 2 points (2,000 fewer): net negative. If instead the tactic's users retain at 25% and the rest is unchanged, the net is positive. &quot;Then inspect the tactic: did it use incentives, forced sharing or hard-to-cancel flows (dark patterns, C10.10) that inflate sign-ups and harm trust? I'd check complaints and the economics: acquisition cost against the lifetime value of the retained users (C20.7). Decision rule: keep the tactic where the 90-day retained users per dollar beat the baseline channel; cut or redesign where they do not; and change the goal metric to retained users, not weekly active users, so growth is not rewarded for churn.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Mix versus real; net retained users; dark patterns; a rule; a better metric.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c27-09\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C27-09</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy-gtm · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C27 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> What is different about building a product in a regulated domain such as health?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;More rules to follow.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names safety, privacy and evidence.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Designs around outcomes, validates claims, engages regulators and clinicians early, handles sensitive data, plans slow cycles, and applies AI-specific evaluation and human oversight.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;Three things change. The goal: outcomes (improved health measures, adherence, fewer adverse events) matter more than engagement; a product that holds attention and doesn't help is a failure. The evidence bar: claims must be supported, and a product that diagnoses or treats may be regulated as a medical device with clinical validation <code>[V: FDA, EU MDR and India's Medical Devices Rules 2017]</code>. The data: health data is among the most sensitive, with strict rules on collection, consent, storage and sharing <code>[V: HHS: HIPAA Privacy Rule and Security Rule apply to covered entities and business associates]</code>. &quot;So I'd involve clinicians, privacy and regulatory experts from the problem stage; define the claim narrowly (information, not diagnosis, unless validated); measure outcomes and safety events; design for clinical workflow, since products that burden clinicians are abandoned; and plan for slow cycles and review before launch. Growth tactics from consumer apps (aggressive notifications, streaks, incentives) can be inappropriate. If AI is involved, I'd add evaluation on real cases with error costs, human oversight for anything consequential, and clear limits shown to users (C24, C25).&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Outcomes; evidence and regulation; sensitive data; clinicians; AI controls.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c28-01\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C28-01</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product-sense · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C28 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Pick a product you use every day and critique it in ten minutes.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;It's great; I'd add more features.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> States the purpose, ranks issues and proposes a change with a metric.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Labels what is verified, inferred and opinion; explains why strengths work; names the cost of the change and what could not be verified.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> <em>(A note-taking app, used as an example; no claim about any real product's internals.)</em> &quot;The app exists to help people capture thoughts quickly and find them later; for the company it serves retention and subscriptions. I'll judge it by how fast I can capture a thought and how reliably I can find it. What works: it opens to a new note, so capture takes one tap (observation), which suits the job because capture is urgent and sorting is not (inference). Ranked issues: first, search misses notes when I misspell (observation, severe for the 'find' job); second, sharing a note requires the other person to sign up (observation); third, I'd like folders to be optional (opinion). My change: forgiving search that handles typos. Metric: the share of searches that end with an opened note within 30 seconds; guardrail: search latency. Cost: indexing and ranking effort. What I can't verify is how many users search at all; I'd check that first.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Purpose; ranked issues with labels; a metric, guardrail and cost.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c28-02\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C28-02</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product-sense · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C28 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> In a critique, what is the difference between an observation, an inference and an opinion? Give an example.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Facts versus feelings.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Defines the three and gives an example.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Applies the layers to a real, sourced example and shows how each is challenged.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;An observation is what I saw or what a source reports. An inference is what I think it implies. An opinion is what I prefer. Example from Spotify's weekly playlist: observation (from the company's reported figures): more than half of listeners returned the next week and saved at least one song; inference: the weekly rhythm builds a habit and saves feed the next list, which also risks narrowing taste; opinion: I'd like a control to steer it. Each is challenged differently. An observation is challenged by asking for the source; an inference by asking for the evidence and the alternative explanation (maybe people return because the list is good, not because of the rhythm); an opinion by asking who else would prefer the opposite.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Clear definitions; a sourced example; how each is tested.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c28-03\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C28-03</h3>\n";
